BEGIN;
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving mill''s technical team debates fabric width shrinkage allowance during marker/pattern planning: a fabric woven at 150cm width on the loom typically finishes (after wet processing/dyeing) at 142-145cm due to relaxation and processing shrinkage. If a garment factory''s pattern is planned assuming a FIXED 150cm usable width (ignoring this known finishing shrinkage), what practical consequence results?', '["No consequence results; fabric width shrinkage during finishing has no relationship to garment pattern/marker planning, making this entire concern technically unfounded","Marker layouts planned against the WRONG (pre-shrinkage) width will not actually fit within the TRUE finished fabric width once it arrives at the cutting room, forcing either a costly marker re-layout (wasting planning time) or, if unnoticed, actual cutting failures/fabric shortages when the marker''s pattern pieces don''t fit the narrower-than-planned finished fabric roll — this connects to the earlier marker-efficiency-cost discussion, since planning against the wrong width baseline can force a less efficient emergency re-layout under time pressure","Fabric width always INCREASES during wet processing/finishing rather than shrinking, making the described 150cm-to-142cm shrinkage scenario technically implausible","Garment factories always cut fabric before any wet processing occurs, making finished-width shrinkage irrelevant to any marker or cutting planning decision"]'::jsonb, 'Marker layouts planned against the WRONG (pre-shrinkage) width will not actually fit within the TRUE finished fabric width once it arrives at the cutting room, forcing either a costly marker re-layout (wasting planning time) or, if unnoticed, actual cutting failures/fabric shortages when the marker''s pattern pieces don''t fit the narrower-than-planned finished fabric roll — this connects to the earlier marker-efficiency-cost discussion, since planning against the wrong width baseline can force a less efficient emergency re-layout under time pressure', 'This connects the fabric width shrinkage principle (loom-state width differs from finished, post-processing width) directly to the earlier marker-efficiency/garment-costing discussion — a marker plan built against the WRONG width assumption (using the loom-state 150cm instead of the true finished ~142-145cm) creates a genuine, costly planning failure: either the discrepancy is caught before cutting (forcing a rushed, likely less-efficient marker re-layout under time pressure, directly costing extra fabric per the earlier marker-efficiency-cost calculation) or, worse, it''s not caught and causes actual cutting/fabric-shortage problems — this is exactly why garment technical teams must plan markers against VERIFIED finished-fabric width specifications, not loom-state width.', 3, '{"fabric-width-shrinkage-marker-planning"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving technologist explains that increasing loom RPM beyond a certain point shows DIMINISHING returns on actual fabric OUTPUT, even before hitting any hard mechanical speed limit, because efficiency% tends to DECLINE as RPM increases (more breaks/stoppages at higher speed). If a loom''s efficiency%×RPM product (a proxy for actual output rate) PEAKS at 800 RPM and then starts declining at higher RPM despite RPM itself still increasing, what does this reveal about the ''push for maximum RPM'' instinct in production planning?', '["Maximum RPM should always be the production target regardless of efficiency%, since RPM alone (not the RPM×efficiency% product) is the only metric that determines actual fabric output rate","Since ACTUAL output is proportional to RPM×efficiency% (not RPM alone), and this product PEAKS at a specific RPM before declining at higher speeds (as efficiency% losses from increased breakage outpace the RPM gain), the mill''s TRUE optimal operating speed is this peak point (800 RPM in this example), not the loom''s absolute maximum mechanical RPM capability — blindly pushing RPM higher past this peak, even if mechanically possible, would actually REDUCE real output despite the higher nominal speed setting, directly illustrating why ''faster machine setting'' doesn''t always mean ''more actual production''","Efficiency% always increases (never decreases) as RPM increases, making the described peak-then-decline pattern in the RPM×efficiency% product technically impossible to occur","RPM and efficiency% are entirely unrelated variables with zero connection to each other, making any described tradeoff between them technically unfounded"]'::jsonb, 'Since ACTUAL output is proportional to RPM×efficiency% (not RPM alone), and this product PEAKS at a specific RPM before declining at higher speeds (as efficiency% losses from increased breakage outpace the RPM gain), the mill''s TRUE optimal operating speed is this peak point (800 RPM in this example), not the loom''s absolute maximum mechanical RPM capability — blindly pushing RPM higher past this peak, even if mechanically possible, would actually REDUCE real output despite the higher nominal speed setting, directly illustrating why ''faster machine setting'' doesn''t always mean ''more actual production''', 'This directly extends the earlier loom-efficiency-vs-RPM-product production calculation principle to a genuine OPTIMIZATION insight — since TRUE output is the PRODUCT of RPM and efficiency% (not RPM in isolation), and efficiency% tends to decline at higher RPM (more breaks/stoppages), there exists a genuine OPTIMAL RPM setting where this product is MAXIMIZED — pushing RPM higher past this peak, even though mechanically possible and superficially appearing ''faster,'' actually REDUCES real output because the efficiency% decline outpaces the RPM gain — this is a sophisticated production-optimization insight directly countering the naive instinct to always run machines at their maximum possible speed setting, and mirrors the earlier bottleneck-improvement-verification lesson about not assuming a locally-impressive-looking change automatically improves overall system output.', 3, '{"optimal-RPM-efficiency-product"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving mill''s technologist explains ''loom state'' (the specific combination of warp tension, shed timing, beat-up force, and take-up rate settings for a given fabric style) must be COMPLETELY re-established whenever switching a loom from one fabric style to a substantially different one. Why can''t a mill simply reuse the PREVIOUS style''s loom settings as a reasonable starting point for a NEW, substantially different fabric construction?', '["Loom settings are always universal and identical across every possible fabric style and construction, making style-specific re-establishment of loom state technically unnecessary in any case","Different fabric constructions (yarn count, EPI/PPI, fiber type) have genuinely different OPTIMAL settings for each individual parameter — as established throughout the weaving domain (optimal warp tension depends on the specific yarn/fabric, shed timing depends on fabric-specific insertion clearance needs, beat-up force depends on target density) — reusing a substantially different style''s settings as-is risks operating FAR from each parameter''s true optimum for the new style, potentially causing exactly the kind of breakage, defect, or efficiency problems each individual optimal-setting discussion throughout this domain has separately established","Reusing previous loom settings for a new fabric style always produces IDENTICAL, perfectly acceptable results regardless of how different the new fabric construction is, making style-specific re-establishment purely a wasted, unnecessary precaution","Loom state settings can only be established once per loom''s entire operational lifetime and can never be changed or re-optimized for different fabric styles under any circumstances"]'::jsonb, 'Different fabric constructions (yarn count, EPI/PPI, fiber type) have genuinely different OPTIMAL settings for each individual parameter — as established throughout the weaving domain (optimal warp tension depends on the specific yarn/fabric, shed timing depends on fabric-specific insertion clearance needs, beat-up force depends on target density) — reusing a substantially different style''s settings as-is risks operating FAR from each parameter''s true optimum for the new style, potentially causing exactly the kind of breakage, defect, or efficiency problems each individual optimal-setting discussion throughout this domain has separately established', 'This synthesizes essentially every individual optimal-parameter discussion from the weaving domain (optimal warp tension, shed timing/angle tradeoffs, beating force vs. density, PPI vs. production rate) into a single unifying insight — since EACH of these parameters has a genuinely different OPTIMAL setting depending on the SPECIFIC fabric construction (yarn count, target density, fiber type), a substantially different new fabric style essentially requires its OWN optimal combination across ALL these parameters simultaneously, and blindly reusing a very different previous style''s settings risks compounding MULTIPLE parameters being simultaneously off from their true individual optimums — which is exactly why professional style changeover procedures involve systematically re-establishing loom state for each new construction rather than treating loom settings as broadly interchangeable across substantially different fabric types.', 3, '{"loom-state-changeover"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
